    field: 'first cabalistic table',
    printed: '',
    used: '',
    why: "The table is a fold-out leaf, and the scan photographed it folded: only its last eleven rows were captured, and the page behind it is blank. The 1823 printing's copy (Google Books qt9SAAAAcAAJ, page 25) was photographed folded too, but further open, and shows rows 57 to 72, the fifty-seventh only in part. Rows 1 to 56 are in no image found. Every column the table carries — name, nation, divine name — is also given in the entry for each genius, so nothing it held is lost, only its arrangement.",
  },
  {
    no: 0,
    kind: 'reconstruction',
    field: 'first cabalistic table, rows 1-56',
    printed: '',
    used: '',
    why: "Rows 62 to 72 are on the reissue's plate, and 57 to 72 on the 1823 printing's; 1 to 56 are in no image found. They are put back from each genius's own entry, which carries the same three columns, and are marked as reconstructed wherever they are shown — never written into the transcription, which is what the page prints. The method can be checked against the rows the plates do show, and the reconstructed table says how often it recovers each column; the misses are places where Lenain's table and his entries differ, such as Mogols for Mongols and Habuiah for Habuhiah. He names his own sources under the table — Kircher's Oedipus Aegyptiacus, tome 2, pages 273 and 287, and Agrippa's Occult Philosophy, book 3, pages 109 and 110, The Hague 1727 — and both have been consulted: Agrippa gives the seventy-two names in the spellings Lenain uses, and Kircher the seventy-two angels by choir in Hebrew and Latin. Neither gives the nations or their names of God, so those two columns rest on Lenain alone.",
  },
